# This repository *is* the upstream: the protocol notes, the render code, the
# tools and the vendor files kept as evidence all live in one tree, and the
# version in debian/changelog is the version of that tree.  There is no upstream
# tarball to diff against, hence 3.0 (native) - see debian/README.source.

Package: xigmatek-connect-lcd
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         ${python3:Depends},
         python3 (>= 3.9),
         python3-pil,
         python3-serial,
         fonts-dejavu-core
Recommends: fonts-liberation,
            xigmatek-connect-lcd-themes (= ${binary:Version})
Suggests: python3-pyqt6, ffmpeg
Description: system stats on the LCD built into Xigmatek Connect AIO coolers
 Unofficial: not affiliated with, endorsed by or approved by XIGMATEK, and
 the program says so on the panel it draws.
 .
 Xigmatek's Connect series all-in-one coolers have a 480x480 LCD in a USB
 port header on the water block, and on Windows the vendor's ALPHA app draws
 CPU and GPU temperatures, clocks and fan speeds on it.  On Linux the panel
 appears as a plain CDC-ACM serial port and nothing talks its protocol; this
 package talks it.
 .
 The `xigmalcd` command is the display loop: it opens /dev/ttyACM0 (or the
 `xigmatek-lcd` symlink this package's udev rule creates), handshakes,
 uploads a PNG frame from Linux sensors at 1-5 fps, optionally uploads an
 MP4 as the panel's background layer, and answers on a control socket so the
 window and `xigmalcd --status` can change what it draws without taking the
 port.  Only one process can hold the port, so that unit is meant to be the
 only opener.
 .
 The window, `xigmaui`, is a client of that socket: theme, clock, canvas,
 scrim, fans, fps, pause, one frame and one background upload, live.  It
 needs PyQt6, which is why PyQt6 is a Suggest rather than a Depends: the
 display loop does not need Qt at all.
 .
 The protocol itself is documented in
 /usr/share/doc/xigmatek-connect-lcd/PROTOCOL.md.gz - measured against the
 panel from Linux, byte by byte, with no vendor documentation of any kind.
 .
 This is an unofficial project, and it ships no automatic repair.  If the
 panel is unhealthy after a boot - fields reading 2147483647, a screen white
 from the first second - the fix is the vendor's own `recovery` request, and
 the only way this package sends it is the command `xigmalcd-reset --yes`,
 run by hand: without --yes it prints the frame and sends nothing.  Nothing
 in this package runs it automatically - not a maintainer script, not the
 unit, and not the control socket unless a loop was deliberately started
 with --allow-reset.

Package: xigmatek-connect-lcd-themes
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: xigmatek-connect-lcd (= ${binary:Version})
Description: ALPHA's own theme library, as shipped, for Xigmatek Connect panels
 Unofficial, and the part of this package that is least ours: these are
 XIGMATEK's files, copied byte for byte out of the vendor's Windows
 installation on the author's bench, and they are redistributed here for the
 record rather than under any licence granted by the rights holder.
 debian/copyright says which files those are and what was actually done to
 obtain them.
 .
 What is in here: the 27 decrypted theme layouts as readable JSON, the 44
 background clips (43 MP4s and one PNG), the 27 baked overlay PNGs, and
 `manifest.json`, whose 125 entries hash those 98 distinct files - the 27
 `background` entries name the same bytes as 27 of the clips - so the copy can
 be checked rather than trusted.  The manifest also records the 27 `.thm`
 containers this package does NOT carry (vendor path, size, sha256): they were
 shipped in 0.1 and 0.2 and are not redistributed from 0.3, being the vendor's
 own encrypted wrapper around media this package already holds unpacked.  See
 themes/alpha/README.md for where the originals come from (the vendor's own
 installer) and how to re-hash this package's record against a copy of them.
 .
 The layouts are what xigmalcd/layout.py reads; the clips sit in their own tree,
 which xigmaui/clips.py scans as one optional clip root (the root constant is
 xigmalcd/main.py:SYSTEM_THEMES).  The window lists clips once per distinct
 file, so installing this package is what turns its picker from the three
 vendored clips into 37 vendor clips plus those three - 40 rows, measured on
 the author's bench out of 161 mp4 files in the roots it walks, the difference
 being the vendor library's own repeats and the Windows app's upload history.
 Removing this package takes the vendor's files away without touching anything
 else.
 .
 Install it to get the vendor's backgrounds and the evidence for every claim
 this project makes about its themes.  Remove it to take them away again:
 nothing else in this package reads them, and the display loop keeps drawing
 its five own themes with it absent.
